## Killip Classification

**Purpose:** Clinical classification of heart failure severity in acute myocardial infarction to estimate in-hospital mortality.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Clinical assessment | Bedside assessment of hemodynamic and pulmonary congestion status | categorical | Class I–IV | Class I = 0; II = 1; III = 2; IV = 3 (classification, not additive) |

**Formula:** `Assign class I–IV based on clinical findings:
Class I: No heart failure, no rales, no S3
Class II: Mild HF, rales in lower lung fields, S3 gallop
Class III: Pulmonary edema
Class IV: Cardiogenic shock (SBP <90 mmHg, signs of hypoperfusion)`

**Score Range:** I–IV

**Interpretation:**

- **I:** No heart failure; in-hospital mortality ~6%
- **II:** Mild HF/rales; mortality ~17%
- **III:** Pulmonary edema; mortality ~38%
- **IV:** Cardiogenic shock; mortality ~67–80%

**Reference:** Killip T 3rd, Kimball JT. Treatment of myocardial infarction in a coronary care unit. A two year experience with 250 patients. Am J Cardiol. 1967;20(4):457-464. PMID: 6059064

**Limitations:** Subjective assessment (inter-rater variability). Derived from 1960s data before modern reperfusion therapy. Does not capture hemodynamic detail (e.g., PCWP, cardiac index). Mortality estimates likely lower in contemporary practice.

