## Braunwald Classification of Unstable Angina

**Purpose:** Classifies unstable angina by severity, clinical circumstance, and intensity of treatment to guide prognosis and management.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Severity class | Clinical severity of angina presentation | categorical | I = new-onset or worsening angina; II = angina at rest within past month but not 48 hours; III = angina at rest within 48 hours | classification only |
| Clinical circumstance | Context in which unstable angina occurs | categorical | A = secondary (extracardiac provocation); B = primary (no extracardiac provocation); C = within 2 weeks of MI | classification only |
| Treatment intensity | Intensity of anti-ischemic therapy at time of angina | categorical | 1 = no or minimal treatment; 2 = standard oral therapy; 3 = maximal IV anti-ischemic therapy | classification only |

**Formula:** `Categorical classification (e.g., IB = primary, new-onset/worsening angina on minimal therapy). Not a numerical score.`

**Score Range:** III-C is highest risk

**Interpretation:**

- **III-C:** Rest angina within 48 hours on maximal IV therapy in post-MI setting — highest risk subgroup
- **I-A:** New-onset/worsening angina secondary to extracardiac cause — lowest risk

**Reference:** Braunwald E. Unstable angina. A classification. Circulation. 1989;80(2):410-414. PMID: 2502287

**Limitations:** Predates the troponin era and modern ACS management. Largely historical — more commonly referenced conceptually than used as a formal scoring tool. Not quantitative.

