## Duke Treadmill Score (DTS)

**Purpose:** Risk stratifies patients undergoing exercise stress testing for coronary artery disease severity and prognosis.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Exercise time | Duration of exercise on standard Bruce protocol | minutes | continuous | ×5 in formula |
| Maximum ST-segment deviation | Maximum ST elevation or depression during exercise or recovery | mm | 0–10+ mm | ×4 (subtracted) |
| Angina index | Angina during test | categorical | 0 = no angina; 1 = non-limiting angina; 2 = exercise-limiting angina | ×1 (subtracted) |

**Formula:** `DTS = Exercise_time − (5 × ST_deviation) − (4 × Angina_index)`

**Score Range:** −25 to +15 (approximately)

**Interpretation:**

- **≥+5:** Low risk: 4-year survival ~99%; annual mortality <0.5%
- **−10 to +4:** Moderate risk: 4-year survival ~95%; annual mortality ~1%
- **≤−11:** High risk: 4-year survival ~79%; annual mortality ~5%

**Reference:** Mark DB, Shaw L, Harrell FE Jr, et al. Prognostic value of a treadmill exercise score in outpatients with suspected coronary artery disease. N Engl J Med. 1991;325(12):849-853. PMID: 1875969

**Limitations:** Requires formal exercise testing (not applicable to patients who cannot exercise). Based on standard Bruce protocol; other protocols require conversion. Not validated with stress imaging modalities. Pre-dates routine use of coronary CTA.

