## Hypertension, Abnormal Renal/Liver Function, Stroke, Bleeding, Labile INRs, Elderly, Drugs or Alcohol Score (HAS-BLED)

**Purpose:** Estimates 1-year risk of major bleeding in patients with atrial fibrillation on anticoagulation, to balance against stroke risk.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hypertension (uncontrolled) | Systolic BP >160 mmHg | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Abnormal renal function | Dialysis, transplant, or serum creatinine >200 µmol/L (>2.26 mg/dL) | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Abnormal liver function | Cirrhosis or bilirubin >2× ULN or AST/ALT/ALP >3× ULN | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Stroke history | Prior ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Bleeding history or predisposition | Prior major bleeding or predisposition (e.g., anemia) | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Labile INRs | Unstable/high INR or poor time in therapeutic range (<60%) | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Elderly (>65 years) | Age greater than 65 | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Drugs | Concomitant antiplatelet agents or NSAIDs | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Alcohol excess | ≥8 drinks per week | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |

**Formula:** `HAS-BLED = Hypertension(1) + Abnormal_renal(1) + Abnormal_liver(1) + Stroke(1) + Bleeding(1) + Labile_INR(1) + Elderly(1) + Drugs(1) + Alcohol(1)`

**Score Range:** 0–9

**Interpretation:**

- **0–2:** Low bleeding risk (~1.0–1.9% annual major bleeding)
- **≥3:** High bleeding risk (~3.7–12.5% annual major bleeding). High score should prompt review of modifiable risk factors, not necessarily contraindicate anticoagulation.

**Reference:** Pisters R, Lane DA, Nieuwlaat R, et al. A novel user-friendly score (HAS-BLED) to assess 1-year risk of major bleeding in patients with atrial fibrillation: the Euro Heart Survey. Chest. 2010;138(5):1093-1100. PMID: 20299623

**Limitations:** Score ≥3 identifies high bleeding risk but should not be used alone to withhold anticoagulation (many high-CHA₂DS₂-VASc patients with HAS-BLED ≥3 still benefit from OAC). Labile INR not applicable to DOAC users. Modest discriminative ability (c-statistic ~0.6–0.7). Some criteria are subjective (bleeding predisposition).

