## Hypertension, Abnormal kidney/liver, Stroke, Bleeding, Excessive alcohol, Reduced platelets, Age, Drugs (HEMORR₂HAGES)

**Purpose:** Estimates bleeding risk in patients with atrial fibrillation on warfarin therapy.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hepatic or renal disease | Hepatic cirrhosis or renal insufficiency (Cr >2.0 or GFR <30) | boolean | Yes/No | 1 each |
| Ethanol abuse | History of alcohol abuse | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Malignancy | Active cancer | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Older age (>75) | Age greater than 75 | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Reduced platelet count or function | Thrombocytopenia or platelet dysfunction | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Re-bleeding (prior bleed) | Prior history of bleeding | boolean | Yes/No | 2 |
| Hypertension (uncontrolled) | Uncontrolled HTN | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Anemia | Hematocrit <30% or Hgb <10 g/dL | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Genetic factors (CYP2C9 polymorphisms) | Known pharmacogenetic variants affecting warfarin metabolism | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Excessive fall risk | History of falls or high fall risk | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |
| Stroke | Prior stroke history | boolean | Yes/No | 1 |

**Formula:** `HEMORR₂HAGES = Hepatic_renal(1) + Ethanol(1) + Malignancy(1) + Older_age(1) + Reduced_platelets(1) + Rebleeding(2) + Hypertension(1) + Anemia(1) + Genetic_factors(1) + Excessive_fall_risk(1) + Stroke(1)`

**Score Range:** 0–12

**Interpretation:**

- **0–1:** Low bleeding risk (~1.9% per year)
- **2–3:** Moderate risk (~2.5–5.4% per year)
- **≥4:** High risk (~8.4–12.3% per year)

**Reference:** Gage BF, Yan Y, Milligan PE, et al. Clinical classification schemes for predicting hemorrhagic results from the National Registry of Atrial Fibrillation (NRAF). Am Heart J. 2006;151(3):713-719. PMID: 16504638

**Limitations:** Complex (11 variables, including genetic testing rarely available). Largely superseded by HAS-BLED due to simplicity. Score components like 'genetic factors' and 'excessive fall risk' are hard to standardize. Developed for warfarin — not validated for DOACs.

