## Reynolds Risk Score (RRS)

**Purpose:** Estimates 10-year risk of cardiovascular events by incorporating family history and hs-CRP in addition to traditional risk factors.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Age | Age in years | years | continuous | coefficient |
| Total cholesterol | Total cholesterol (mg/dL) | mg/dL | continuous | coefficient |
| HDL-C | HDL cholesterol (mg/dL) | mg/dL | continuous | coefficient |
| Systolic BP | Systolic blood pressure (mmHg) | mmHg | continuous | coefficient |
| Smoking | Current smoker | boolean | Yes/No | coefficient |
| hs-CRP | High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (mg/L) | mg/L | continuous (log-transformed) | ln(hsCRP) × coefficient |
| Family history of premature CVD | MI or stroke in first-degree male relative <55 or female <65 | boolean | Yes/No | coefficient |
| HbA1c (if diabetic) | Glycated hemoglobin, used in diabetic subgroup model | % | continuous | coefficient (diabetic model only) |

**Formula:** `Cox proportional hazards model: 10-year risk = 1 − S₀(10)^exp(β₁×ln(age) + β₂×ln(TC) + β₃×ln(HDL) + β₄×ln(SBP) + β₅×smoker + β₆×ln(hsCRP) + β₇×FH). Sex-specific coefficients. S₀(10) is baseline 10-year survival. Exact coefficients published in Ridker PM, et al. 2007/2008. Calculator available at reynoldsriskscore.org.`

**Score Range:** 0–100% (10-year risk)

**Interpretation:**

- **<5%:** Low risk
- **5–10%:** Intermediate risk
- **10–20%:** Intermediate-high risk
- **>20%:** High risk

**Reference:** Ridker PM, Buring JE, Rifai N, Cook NR. Development and validation of improved algorithms for the assessment of global cardiovascular risk in women: the Reynolds Risk Score. JAMA. 2007;297(6):611-619. PMID: 17299196. Male model: Ridker PM, et al. Circulation. 2008;118(22):2243-2251. PMID: 18997196

**Limitations:** Requires hs-CRP (not universally measured) and family history data. Re-classification benefit over Framingham is modest. Not widely incorporated into US guidelines (PCE preferred). hs-CRP has significant biological variability.

