## Simplified Acute Physiology Score II (SAPS II)

**Purpose:** Predicts ICU mortality using physiological variables, type of admission, and underlying diagnosis collected within the first 24 hours of ICU admission.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Age | Age in years | years | <40=0; 40–59=7; 60–69=12; 69–74=15; ≥75=18 | 0–18 |
| Heart rate | Heart rate (bpm) | bpm | <40=11; 40–69=2; 70–119=0; 120–159=4; ≥160=7 | 0–11 |
| Systolic BP | Systolic blood pressure (mmHg) | mmHg | <70=13; 70–99=5; 100–199=0; ≥200=2 | 0–13 |
| Body temperature | Core temperature (°C) | °C | <39=0; ≥39=3 | 0–3 |
| PaO₂/FiO₂ | Oxygenation ratio | mmHg | <100=11; 100–199=9; ≥200=0 (or if no ABG, use 0) | 0–11 |
| Urine output | 24-hour urine output | L/day | <0.5=11; 0.5–0.99=4; ≥1.0=0 | 0–11 |
| Serum urea/BUN | Blood urea nitrogen | mmol/L | <10=0; 10–29=6; ≥30=10 | 0–10 |
| WBC | White blood cell count (×10⁹/L) | ×10⁹/L | <1=12; 1–19=0; ≥20=3 | 0–12 |
| Serum potassium | Potassium (mEq/L) | mEq/L | <3=3; 3–4.9=0; ≥5=3 | 0–3 |
| Serum sodium | Sodium (mEq/L) | mEq/L | <125=5; 125–144=0; ≥145=1 | 0–5 |
| Serum bicarbonate | HCO₃⁻ (mEq/L) | mEq/L | <15=6; 15–19=3; ≥20=0 | 0–6 |
| Bilirubin | Total bilirubin (mg/dL) | mg/dL | <4=0; 4–5.9=4; ≥6=9 | 0–9 |
| GCS | Glasgow Coma Scale | score | 3–15 | 26 − GCS |
| Chronic disease | Metastatic cancer, hematologic malignancy, or AIDS | boolean | AIDS=17; metastatic cancer=9; hematologic malignancy=10 | 0–17 |
| Type of admission | Surgical status | categorical | scheduled surgery=0; medical=6; emergency surgery=8 | 0–8 |

**Formula:** `SAPS II = Sum of all variable points (see categories above). Predicted mortality = e^(logit) / (1 + e^(logit)), where logit = −7.7631 + 0.0737 × SAPS_II + 0.9971 × ln(SAPS_II + 1)`

**Score Range:** 0–163

**Interpretation:**

- **0–20:** Low predicted ICU mortality (<10%)
- **20–40:** Moderate predicted mortality (10–30%)
- **40–60:** High predicted mortality (30–60%)
- **≥60:** Very high predicted mortality (>60%)

**Reference:** Le Gall JR, Lemeshow S, Saulnier F. A new Simplified Acute Physiology Score (SAPS II) based on a European/North American multicenter study. JAMA. 1993;270(24):2957-2963. PMID: 8254858

**Limitations:** Overestimates mortality in modern ICUs (calibrated to early 1990s data). Requires 24 hours of data. Diagnostic categories are complex. Superseded by SAPS 3 which uses data from the hour before ICU admission.

---

## Simplified Acute Physiology Score 3 (SAPS 3)

**Purpose:** Predicts ICU and hospital mortality using data from the hour before and first hour of ICU admission, with improved global calibration.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Demographics | Age, sex, comorbidities before ICU admission, use of major therapeutic options before ICU, location before ICU, length of stay before ICU, surgical status | mixed | various | various points per variable |
| Physiological variables at ICU admission | GCS, HR, SBP, temperature, bilirubin, creatinine, WBC, platelets, pH, PaO₂/FiO₂ or SpO₂/FiO₂, potassium, bicarbonate, sodium | mixed | various ranges with point assignments | various points per variable |

**Formula:** `SAPS 3 = Sum of all item scores (see original publication for full scoring table). Hospital mortality = 1/(1 + e^(-ln(SAPS3/100-SAPS3))). Three different regression models for three geographic regions (Europe/Americas/Australasia, Asia, Middle East).`

**Score Range:** 0–217

**Interpretation:**

- **Low SAPS 3:** Low predicted hospital mortality
- **High SAPS 3:** High predicted hospital mortality

**Reference:** Moreno RP, Metnitz PGH, Almeida E, et al. SAPS 3 — from evaluation of the patient to evaluation of the intensive care unit. Part 2: development of a prognostic model for hospital outcome at ICU admission. Intensive Care Med. 2005;31(10):1345-1355. PMID: 16132892

**Limitations:** Complex scoring requiring many variables. Three different calibration regions. Requires specific variables that may not be available in all settings. Some variables (e.g., reason for ICU admission) require coding. Superseded in some settings by dynamic models using machine learning.

---

## Sequential Organ Failure Assessment Score (SOFA)

**Purpose:** Quantifies the degree of organ failure across six organ systems; used to track organ dysfunction over time in critically ill patients and to define sepsis (with suspected infection).

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Respiratory (PaO₂/FiO₂) | Oxygenation ratio | mmHg | ≥400=0; <400=1; <300=2; <200+vent=3; <100+vent=4 | 0–4 |
| Coagulation (platelets) | Platelet count (×10³/µL) | ×10³/µL | ≥150=0; <150=1; <100=2; <50=3; <20=4 | 0–4 |
| Liver (bilirubin) | Total bilirubin (mg/dL) | mg/dL | <1.2=0; 1.2–1.9=1; 2.0–5.9=2; 6.0–11.9=3; ≥12.0=4 | 0–4 |
| Cardiovascular (MAP or vasopressors) | Mean arterial pressure and vasopressor requirement | categorical | MAP≥70=0; MAP<70=1; dopamine≤5 or dobutamine=2; dopamine>5 or epi≤0.1 or norepi≤0.1=3; dopamine>15 or epi>0.1 or norepi>0.1=4 | 0–4 |
| CNS (GCS) | Glasgow Coma Scale | score | 15=0; 13–14=1; 10–12=2; 6–9=3; <6=4 | 0–4 |
| Renal (creatinine or urine output) | Serum creatinine (mg/dL) or urine output (mL/day) | mg/dL or mL/day | Cr<1.2=0; 1.2–1.9=1; 2.0–3.4=2; 3.5–4.9 or UO<500mL/d=3; Cr≥5.0 or UO<200mL/d=4 | 0–4 |

**Formula:** `SOFA = Respiratory(0-4) + Coagulation(0-4) + Liver(0-4) + Cardiovascular(0-4) + CNS(0-4) + Renal(0-4)`

**Score Range:** 0–24

**Interpretation:**

- **0–6:** Low organ dysfunction
- **7–9:** Moderate organ dysfunction
- **10–12:** Severe organ dysfunction; high ICU mortality (~50%)
- **≥13:** Very severe organ dysfunction; ICU mortality >80%
- **ΔSOFA ≥2 from baseline in setting of infection:** Sepsis per Sepsis-3 definition (Singer 2016)

**Reference:** Vincent JL, Moreno R, Takala J, et al. The SOFA (Sepsis-related Organ Failure Assessment) score to describe organ dysfunction/failure. Intensive Care Med. 1996;22(7):707-710. PMID: 8844239

**Limitations:** Not designed as a mortality prediction tool (descriptive of organ dysfunction). Vasopressor scoring requires knowledge of specific agent and dose. Some components (e.g., GCS) may be affected by sedation. Does not account for trends unless calculated serially. Different laboratories and definitions (e.g., for dopamine dosing) may affect scoring.

---

## Quick Sequential Organ Failure Assessment (qSOFA)

**Purpose:** Bedside screening tool to identify patients with suspected infection who are at risk for poor outcomes (prolonged ICU stay or in-hospital mortality). Part of Sepsis-3 definition.

**Input Variables:**

| Variable | Description | Unit | Range/Options | Points |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Respiratory rate ≥22/min | Respiratory rate at presentation | breaths/min | ≥22 = 1 point | 0 or 1 |
| Altered mentation | GCS <15 | boolean | Yes = 1 point | 0 or 1 |
| Systolic BP ≤100 mmHg | Systolic blood pressure | mmHg | ≤100 = 1 point | 0 or 1 |

**Formula:** `qSOFA = RR≥22(1) + GCS<15(1) + SBP≤100(1)`

**Score Range:** 0–3

**Interpretation:**

- **0–1:** Low risk of poor outcome from infection
- **≥2:** High risk. Consider further assessment for sepsis, including full SOFA score and clinical judgment.

**Reference:** Singer M, Deutschman CS, Seymour CW, et al. The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3). JAMA. 2016;315(8):801-810. PMID: 26903338

**Limitations:** Not intended for use as a diagnostic criterion for sepsis — it is a screening/prognostic tool. Low sensitivity for sepsis (misses many septic patients). Not intended for use in the ICU. Better at identifying patients at risk of poor outcome than identifying sepsis per se. Does not perform as well as SIRS in some settings.

